in's), leukemia (lymphoid), cancer/tumors (unspecified), and multiple myeloma.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB06174","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","items":[{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","text":"Antitussive Agents","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3372","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"},{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","text":"ANTITUSSIVE AGENT WHICH DEPRESSES MEDULLARY CENTERS & SUPPRESSES COUGH REFLEX. IT IS USED IN MGMNT OF COUGH IN BRONCHIAL ASTHMA & PULMONARY EMPHYSEMA. DRUG REDUCES FREQUENCY & INTENSITY OF COUGHING PAROXYSMS.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3372","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"},{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","text":"DRUG HAS NO MORPHINE-LIKE EFFECTS & HAS NO EFFECT ON MORPHNE ABSTINENCE SYNDROME. TOLERANCE TO ANTITUSSIVE EFFECT HAS NOT BEEN OBSERVED. NOSCAPINE IS NO LONGER CONTROLLED UNDER FEDERAL NARCOTIC LAW.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3372","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"},{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","text":"NOSCAPINE HAS NO ANALGESIC PROPERTIES OR DEPENDENCE LIABILITY.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3372","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"}]},{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","items":[{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","text":"Agents that suppress cough. They act centrally on the medullary cough center. EXPECTORANTS, also used in the treatment of cough, act locally.","source":"Medical Subject Headings (MeSH)","url":"https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0001516.html","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Mechanism of Action","items":[{"section":"Mechanism of Action","text":"Noscapine's antitussive effects appear to be primarily mediated by its sigma receptor agonist activity. Evidence for this mechanism is suggested by experimental evidence in rats. Pretreatment with rimcazole, a sigma specific antagonist, causes a dose-dependent reduction in antitussive activity of noscapine.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB06174","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Mechanism of Action","text":"...EXCEPT FOR ITS ANTITUSSIVE EFFECT, IT HAS NO SIGNIFICANT ACTIONS ON CNS IN DOSES WITHIN THERAPEUTIC RANGE.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3372","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"}]},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","items":[{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"ITS ANTITUSSIVE POTENCY & ONSET & DURATION OF ACTION ARE APPROX EQUAL, MG FOR MG, TO THOSE OF CODEINE.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3372","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"}]},{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","items":[{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","text":"MECONIN WAS THE MAJOR URINARY METABOLITE OF RATS, RABBITS, & HUMANS WHO HAD RECEIVED 120, 150 MG/KG & 10 MG, RESPECTIVELY, ORALLY. IT ACCOUNTED FOR APPROX 3, 8, & 2% OF DOSE RESPECTIVELY, AFTER 24 HR. O-DEMETHYLATED METABOLITES WERE ALSO FOUND.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3372","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"}]}],"safety":[],"manufacturing":[{"section":"Methods of Manufacturing","items":[{"section":"Methods of Manufacturing","text":"MAILLARD, US PATENT 3,108,106 (1963 TO JACQUES LOGEAIS).","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/3372"}]},{"sect